14: # TypeScript Quality Standards
15: 
16: Reach for this file when a TypeScript type choice, error boundary or tsconfig setting needs a defensible default rather than a local preference.
17: 
18: ---
19: 
20: ## 1. OVERVIEW
21: 
22: ### Purpose
23: 
24: Establishes type system patterns, error handling, documentation, async patterns, and tsconfig baseline that all TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment must follow.
25: 
26: ### When to Use
27: 
28: - Writing new TypeScript modules
29: - Reviewing TypeScript code for quality compliance
30: - Setting up type safety, error handling, and documentation patterns
31: 
32: ---
33: 
34: ## 2. INTERFACE VS TYPE DECISION GUIDE
35: 
36: ### When to Use `interface`
37: 
38: Use `interface` for object shapes, class contracts, and data structures:
39: 
40: ```typescript
41: // Object shapes
42: interface SearchResult {
43:   readonly id: string;
44:   score: number;
45:   text: string;
46:   metadata: Record<string, unknown>;
47: }
48: 
49: // Class contracts
50: interface EmbeddingProvider {
51:   embed(text: string): Promise<number[]>;
52:   batchEmbed(texts: string[]): Promise<number[][]>;
53:   getDimension(): number;
54:   getModelName(): string;
55:   isReady(): boolean;
56: }
57: 
58: // Extending shapes
59: interface DetailedSearchResult extends SearchResult {
60:   highlights: string[];
61:   explanation: string;
62: }
63: ```
64: 
65: **Why interfaces for objects**: Interfaces support declaration merging, provide clearer error messages, and signal "this is a data contract" to the reader.
66: 
67: ### When to Use `type`
68: 
69: Use `type` for unions, intersections, mapped types, conditional types, and function signatures:
70: 
71: ```typescript
72: // Union types
73: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived' | 'pending' | 'deleted';
74: 
75: // Intersection types
76: type Timestamped<T> = T & { createdAt: number; updatedAt: number };
77: 
78: // Function signatures
79: type ScoreCalculator = (input: number, weight: number) => number;
80: 
81: // Conditional types
82: type AsyncReturn<T> = T extends Promise<infer U> ? U : T;
83: 
84: // Mapped types
85: type Nullable<T> = { [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null };
86: 
87: // Tuple types
88: type Coordinates = [x: number, y: number];
89: ```
90: 
91: ### Decision Summary
92: 
93: | Use Case                           | Construct   | Reason                          |
94: |------------------------------------|-------------|---------------------------------|
95: | Object shapes / data structures    | `interface` | Extensible, clear error messages|
96: | Class contracts                    | `interface` | Supports `implements`           |
97: | Union types                        | `type`      | Only `type` supports unions     |
98: | Intersection / mapped / conditional| `type`      | Only `type` supports these      |
99: | Function signatures (standalone)   | `type`      | Cleaner syntax than interface    |
100: | Simple primitives / literals       | `type`      | `type ID = string`              |
101: 
102: ---
103: 
104: ## 3. TYPE SAFETY POLICIES
105: 
106: ### `unknown` Over `any`
107: 
108: **Policy**: Use `unknown` instead of `any` in public API surfaces. The `any` type disables type checking entirely. `unknown` forces callers to narrow the type before use.
109: 
110: ```typescript
111: // CORRECT — unknown forces type narrowing
112: function parseInput(raw: unknown): SearchConfig {
113:   if (typeof raw !== 'object' || raw === null) {
114:     throw new TypeError('Expected object input');
115:   }
116:   const obj = raw as Record<string, unknown>;
117:   // validate and narrow further...
118:   return obj as SearchConfig;
119: }
120: 
121: // INCORRECT — any disables all type checking
122: function parseInput(raw: any): SearchConfig {
123:   return raw;  // No validation, no safety
124: }
125: ```
126: 
127: **Permitted `any` usage** (must include justification comment):
128: 
129: ```typescript
130: // Interop with untyped third-party library (sqlite-vec native module)
131: import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
132: 
133: const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
134: 
135: // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
136: const sqliteVec = require('sqlite-vec') as any;
137: ```
138: 
139: ### Strict Null Checks
140: 
141: With `strictNullChecks: true`, `null` and `undefined` must be handled explicitly:
142: 
143: ```typescript
144: // CORRECT — explicit null handling
145: function findMemory(id: string): MemoryRecord | null {
146:   const record = database.get(id);
147:   if (!record) return null;
148:   return record;
149: }
150: 
151: // Usage requires null check
152: const memory = findMemory('abc');
153: if (memory !== null) {
154:   console.log(memory.text);  // TypeScript knows memory is not null here
155: }
156: ```
157: 
158: ### Non-Null Assertions
159: 
160: **Policy**: Avoid non-null assertions (`!`). When absolutely necessary, include a justification comment:
161: 
162: ```typescript
163: // AVOID — non-null assertion hides potential bugs
164: const name = user!.name;
165: 
166: // PREFERRED — explicit null check
167: if (user === null) throw new Error('User not found');
168: const name = user.name;
169: 
170: // PERMITTED — with justification
171: // Process guarantees element exists after validation phase (see line 42)
172: const element = document.getElementById('root')!;
173: ```
174: 
175: ### Generic Constraints
176: 
177: Use `extends` to constrain generic type parameters:
178: 
179: ```typescript
180: // CORRECT — constrained generic
181: function getProperty<T extends Record<string, unknown>, K extends keyof T>(
182:   obj: T,
183:   key: K,
184: ): T[K] {
185:   return obj[key];
186: }
187: 
188: // CORRECT — constraint ensures required shape
189: interface HasId { readonly id: string; }
190: function findById<T extends HasId>(items: T[], id: string): T | undefined {
191:   return items.find(item => item.id === id);
192: }
193: 
194: // INCORRECT — unconstrained when constraint is needed
195: function getProperty<T>(obj: T, key: string): unknown {
196:   return (obj as Record<string, unknown>)[key];  // Loses type safety
197: }
198: ```
199: 
200: ---
201: 
202: ## 4. DISCRIMINATED UNIONS
203: 
204: Use discriminated unions for state management where an object can be in one of several distinct states:
205: 
206: ```typescript
207: // Discriminated union for operation results
208: interface SuccessResult<T> {
209:   readonly success: true;
210:   readonly data: T;
211: }
212: 
213: interface ErrorResult {
214:   readonly success: false;
215:   readonly error: string;
216:   readonly code: string;
217: }
218: 
219: type OperationResult<T> = SuccessResult<T> | ErrorResult;
220: 
221: // Usage — TypeScript narrows automatically
222: function handleResult(result: OperationResult<SearchResult[]>): void {
223:   if (result.success) {
224:     // TypeScript knows: result.data exists
225:     console.log(`Found ${result.data.length} results`);
226:   } else {
227:     // TypeScript knows: result.error and result.code exist
228:     console.error(`Error ${result.code}: ${result.error}`);
229:   }
230: }
231: ```
232: 
233: ### State Machine Pattern
234: 
235: ```typescript
236: type ConnectionState =
237:   | { status: 'disconnected' }
238:   | { status: 'connecting'; attempt: number }
239:   | { status: 'connected'; connection: DatabaseConnection }
240:   | { status: 'error'; error: Error; lastAttempt: number };
241: 
242: function getConnectionInfo(state: ConnectionState): string {
243:   switch (state.status) {
244:     case 'disconnected':
245:       return 'Not connected';
246:     case 'connecting':
247:       return `Connecting (attempt ${state.attempt})`;
248:     case 'connected':
249:       return `Connected to ${state.connection.host}`;
250:     case 'error':
251:       return `Error: ${state.error.message}`;
252:   }
253: }
254: ```
255: 
256: ---
257: 
258: ## 5. UTILITY TYPES
259: 
260: ### Standard TypeScript Utility Types
261: 
262: Use built-in utility types instead of reinventing them:
263: 
264: | Utility Type       | Purpose                                    | Example                                     |
265: |--------------------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
266: | `Partial<T>`      | All properties optional                    | `Partial<SearchConfig>` for update payloads  |
267: | `Required<T>`     | All properties required                    | `Required<OptionalConfig>` for validated config |
268: | `Pick<T, K>`      | Select specific properties                 | `Pick<MemoryRecord, 'id' \| 'text'>`        |
269: | `Omit<T, K>`      | Exclude specific properties                | `Omit<MemoryRecord, 'internalScore'>`        |
270: | `Record<K, V>`    | Object with typed keys and values          | `Record<string, unknown>` for metadata       |
271: | `Readonly<T>`     | All properties readonly                    | `Readonly<SearchConfig>` for immutable config |
272: | `ReadonlyArray<T>`| Immutable array                            | `ReadonlyArray<string>` or `readonly string[]`|
273: | `NonNullable<T>`  | Exclude null and undefined                 | `NonNullable<string \| null>` = `string`     |
274: | `ReturnType<T>`   | Extract function return type               | `ReturnType<typeof calculateScore>`          |
275: | `Parameters<T>`   | Extract function parameter types as tuple  | `Parameters<typeof search>`                  |
276: 
277: ### Common Patterns in OpenCode
278: 
279: ```typescript
280: // Configuration with optional overrides
281: function configure(overrides: Partial<SearchConfig>): SearchConfig {
282:   return { ...DEFAULT_CONFIG, ...overrides };
283: }
284: 
285: // Immutable configuration
286: const FROZEN_CONFIG = Object.freeze({
287:   maxResults: 100,
288:   timeout: 5000,
289:   includeMetadata: true,
290: } satisfies SearchConfig);
291: 
292: // Metadata as untyped key-value pairs
293: interface MemoryRecord {
294:   readonly id: string;
295:   text: string;
296:   metadata: Record<string, unknown>;
297: }
298: 
299: // Create type from existing interface
300: type MemorySummary = Pick<MemoryRecord, 'id' | 'text'>;
301: type PublicMemory = Omit<MemoryRecord, 'internalScore'>;
302: ```
303: 
304: ---
305: 
306: ## 6. RETURN TYPE ANNOTATIONS
307: 
308: ### Policy: Explicit for Public API, Inferred for Private Helpers
309: 
310: ```typescript
311: // PUBLIC API — explicit return type (required by P1 checklist)
312: export function calculateDecayScore(
313:   baseScore: number,
314:   ageInHours: number,
315: ): number {
316:   return baseScore * Math.exp(-0.1 * ageInHours);
317: }
318: 
319: // PUBLIC API — async explicit return type
320: export async function searchMemories(
321:   query: string,
322:   options: SearchOptions,
323: ): Promise<SearchResult[]> {
324:   // implementation
325: }
326: 
327: // PRIVATE HELPER — inferred return type is acceptable
328: function normalizeScore(raw: number, max: number) {
329:   return Math.min(raw / max, 1.0);
330: }
331: ```
332: 
333: **Why explicit on public API**: Return types serve as documentation, prevent accidental signature changes, and improve compile-time error messages for consumers.
334: 
335: **Why inferred on private helpers**: Reduces boilerplate for internal code where the return type is obvious from the implementation.
336: 
337: ---
